<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the hardened runtime has to be asked to allow.

  Exactly one thing, and it is here because of extensions. A component is
  WebAssembly, and running it means compiling it to arm64 at load and jumping
  into the result. The hardened runtime's whole point is that a signed app does
  not execute code nobody signed, so without this the kernel kills the process
  the instant wasmtime enters the code it just generated:

      Exception Type:      EXC_BAD_ACCESS (SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid))
      Termination Reason:  Namespace CODESIGNING, Code 2, Invalid Page

  It is `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` rather than `allow-jit` because of
  how wasmtime maps that memory. `allow-jit` permits only regions mapped with
  `MAP_JIT`, and wasmtime's unix backend uses an ordinary `mmap` followed by
  `mprotect` to make it executable — which `allow-jit` does not cover. The
  crash report shows it: the faulting page is a plain `VM_ALLOCATE r-x` region.

  This is a real reduction in what the hardened runtime is protecting against,
  and it applies to the whole process rather than to the extension host. It
  buys running extensions at all. The alternative that does not need it is
  wasmtime's Pulley interpreter, which executes WebAssembly without generating
  native code at the cost of running it far slower; that is a decision about
  the extension system rather than about packaging, and it is not made here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
